[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1
diagnosis_text:
No evidence of an aswa2 hardware fault. All three aswa2 ports show clean counters and no drops (ip -s link on eth0/eth1/eth2: errors 0, dropped 0), stable STP (eth1 forwarding, eth2 blocking, no topology changes), and a stable MAC table. The default gateway MAC for VLAN 10 (36:f8:7d:70:8c:a7) is consistently learned on aswa2 eth1, and PC1’s MAC (66:50:ca:99:b2:c2) is on aswa2 eth0. Live pings from PC1 and PC2 to 10.1.0.1 succeed with low RTT when tested.

The anomaly is at the distribution layer: on dswa2 the VLAN 10 SVI is UP and hosts the VIP 10.1.0.1/32 (ip -br addr br0.10 shows 10.1.0.3/24 and 10.1.0.1/32), while on dswa1 the VLAN 10 SVI is DOWN (ip -br addr shows br0.10 DOWN 10.1.0.2/24). With aswa2’s active uplink toward dswa1 (eth1 forwarding; eth2 to dswa2 blocking), VLAN 10 traffic from A2 must hairpin across the dswa1<->dswa2 LACP peer link to reach the active gateway on dswa2. During MAC aging or any brief topology events, initial ARP/ICMP can appear intermittently slow/unresponsive, matching the reported symptoms, while VLAN 40 and 99 remain unaffected.

Root cause: dswa1’s VLAN 10 SVI is administratively down/misconfigured, leaving 10.1.0.1 active only on dswa2 and forcing a suboptimal path. Corrective fix: bring up dswa1 br0.10 and restore the intended first-hop redundancy (VRRP/HSRP) for 10.1.0.1, or adjust STP so A2’s active uplink points directly to the active gateway (dswa2). No replacement of aswa2 or its ports is warranted. === END ===